WebFluxSseServerTransportProvider.class  must  put  package io.modelcontextprotocol.server.transport;

The correct way of accessing request-level information is through the `contextExtractor`([docs](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/api/mcp/mcp-server-boot-starter-docs.html#_adding_data_to_mcptransportcontext)) and `McpTransportContext` ([docs](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/api/mcp/mcp-annotations-special-params.html#_mcptransportcontext)).

See examples in #5374:

```java
@Bean
public WebMvcStreamableServerTransportProvider transport(/* ... */) {

	return WebMvcStreamableServerTransportProvider.builder()
		// ...
		.contextExtractor(serverRequest -> McpTransportContext.create(Map.of("headers", serverRequest.headers())))
		.build();
}
```

The specific extractor depends on your transport stack (Servlet, WebMvc or WebFlux). You can then consume the context in the tool:

```java
@McpTool(name = "foo", description = "fooes the bar")
public String foo(McpSyncServerExchange exchange, /* ... */) {
	var context = exchange.transportContext();
	// ...
}
```

