```java
@Tool(description = "Delete the book by id")
@AiyoGated(actionType = "book_mutation", reasonTemplate = "Delete book {bookId}?")
public void deleteBookById(String bookId) {
    // Runs only on ALLOW. On ESCALATE / BLOCK the method body never executes.
}
```

The verdict is enforced by a `RuntimeDecisionPoint` bean evaluating a policy document; verdicts are `ALLOW` / `ESCALATE` / `BLOCK`. `BlockedByPolicy` and `EscalationRequired` are catchable exceptions in your controller or Spring AI advisor — your existing manual-execution loop can route `EscalationRequired` to whatever approval UI you have, with the pending receipt digest binding the eventual approval back to the exact tool+args pair that was paused (which is the thing elicitation's stateless timeout can't give you).

Repo: https://github.com/aiyoplane/aiyoplane-spring-ai — happy to answer questions here or there if the shape is useful. If a native Spring AI `@RequiresConsent` or `ToolApprovalStrategy` ships later, migration should be a package rename; the API deliberately follows the proposed shape in #4878 and the annotation shape in #3848 so the two sit alongside, not against each other.

