@quaff Access tokens have a limited TTL. Since they expire, I don't think they're a great candidate for configuration. I do see the point in having custom "default" headers, though.

Are you aware of the work that we've done in https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/java-sdk/pull/388 ?
We're integrating it in https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-ai/pull/3994, with the ultimate goal to provide OAuth2 integration. This opens a much broader field, where users can customize the entire MCP client HTTP request for HttpClient-based transport.

> Access tokens have a limited TTL. Since they expire, I don't think they're a great candidate for configuration. 

We could create long live or never expired access token like GitHub Personal Access Token, https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server

```json
{
  "servers": {
    "github": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer ${input:github_mcp_pat}"
      }
    }
  },
  "inputs": [
    {
      "type": "promptString",
      "id": "github_mcp_pat",
      "description": "GitHub Personal Access Token",
      "password": true
    }
  ]
}
```


> I do see the point in having custom "default" headers, though.

I think it's reasonable to configure custom headers.

@dvag-joerg-winter out-of-band acquired access token should even be possible in the 1.0.x line.

If you are using `spring-ai-starter-mcp-client` without WebFlux, then provide your own SSE transport as a bean (and probably exclude the [auto-configuration](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-ai/blob/1.0.x/auto-configurations/mcp/spring-ai-autoconfigure-mcp-client/src/main/java/org/springframework/ai/mcp/client/autoconfigure/SseHttpClientTransportAutoConfiguration.java)), and set a custom `HttpRequest.Builder` in that transport (see [MCP SseTransport](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/java-sdk/blob/6307f0697e95ee634dc53dbc8cb5845e2e3bf067/mcp/src/main/java/io/modelcontextprotocol/client/transport/HttpClientSseClientTransport.java#L282-L291)). That request builder can have a default header. Something like:

```java
HttpRequest.newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer " + yourToken);
```

If you're using `WebClient` with `spring-ai-starter-mcp-client-webflux`, provide your own `WebClient.Builder` bean, which will be picked up by the [auto-configuration](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-ai/blob/1.0.x/auto-configurations/mcp/spring-ai-autoconfigure-mcp-client/src/main/java/org/springframework/ai/mcp/client/autoconfigure/SseWebFluxTransportAutoConfiguration.java). Something like:

```java
@Bean
public WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder() {
    return WebClient.builder()
            .defaultHeader("Authorization", "Bearer " + yourToken);
}
```


Starting with 1.1.0, for HttpClient (`spring-ai-starter-mcp-client`), you can also use the dynamic `McpSyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer` (see [MCP docs](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/sdk/java/mcp-client#streamable-http-httpclient), in Client Transport > HttpClient). If you provide it as a bean, it will be picked up by Spring AI.

@dvag-joerg-winter 

>  Would parsing the jsonrpc & deciding based on the 'method' value, which (out-of-band aquired) token to use, be the best/recommended way?

That's one possibility, yes. That's why we provided the body of the request.
However, specifically in Spring, you can check that the `RequestContextHolder` contains the current request ; or that the `SecurityContext` contains an authentication. This is a good proxy to verify whether a user is involved or not. I'm working on it in springai-community/mcp-security ([example](https://github.com/spring-ai-community/mcp-security/blob/f39f73105adef95df8b52491dc54f59402b6297e/mcp-client-security/src/main/java/org/springaicommunity/mcp/security/client/sync/oauth2/http/client/OAuth2HybridSyncHttpRequestCustomizer.java)).

>  So the remaining question is, how the authorization_token of the host application can be accessed from my McpSyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer? Would that be via McpTransportContext?

Correct. You'd use something like [this snippet](https://github.com/spring-ai-community/mcp-security/blob/f39f73105adef95df8b52491dc54f59402b6297e/mcp-client-security/src/main/java/org/springaicommunity/mcp/security/client/sync/AuthenticationMcpTransportContextProvider.java), then you can plug it in your client:

```java
@Bean
McpSyncClientCustomizer syncClientCustomizer() {
	return (name, syncSpec) -> syncSpec.transportContextProvider(new AuthenticationMcpTransportContextProvider());
}
```

To add headers for which you know the value in advance, it is doable with a single bean:

1. For web-client based support, provide your own `WebClient.Builder`:

```java
@Bean
WebClient.Builder mcpClient() {
    return WebClient.builder().defaultHeader("x-custom-header", "<HEADER-VALUE>");
}
```

2. For HTTP-client based, provide `McpSyncRequestCustomizer`:

```java
@Bean
McpSyncHttpClientRequestCustomizer requestCustomizer() {
    return (builder, method, endpoint, body, context) -> builder.header("x-custom-header", "<HEADER-VALUE>");
}
```

The following code is valid for Spring AI version 1.1.2.

If you are using `spring-ai-starter-mcp-client`:

```java
// 1. Build the Transport and add dynamic headers
HttpClientStreamableHttpTransport transport = HttpClientStreamableHttpTransport
    .builder("http://your-mcp-server.com") // MCP server address
    .endpoint("/api") // Endpoint path
    .customizeRequest(builder -> {
        // Dynamically compute header values here
        String dynamicValue = calculateDynamicHeader(); // Your dynamic computation logic
        builder.header("X-Custom-Header", dynamicValue);
        // You can add multiple headers
        builder.header("Authorization", "Bearer " + getDynamicToken());
    })
    .build();

// 2. Build the MCP client
McpSyncClient mcpSyncClient = McpClient.sync(transport)
    .requestTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(60))
    .build();

// 3. Build the ChatClient
ChatClient.create(chatModel)
    .toolCallbacks(SyncMcpToolCallbackProvider.builder().mcpClients(mcpSyncClient).build())
    .user(message)
    .stream()
    .content();
```

If you are using `spring-ai-starter-mcp-client-webflux`, the approach is similar, but the class names differ:

```java
