```

I implemented a work around as:

```java

private static final Object SCHEMA_LOCK = new Object();

    private <T> T callModelForEntity(ChatClient chatClient, String chatId, Class<T> clazz, String userMessage) {
        Message systemMsg = SystemMessage.builder()
                .text(PROCESS_STATEMENT_SYSTEM_PROMPT).build();

        PromptTemplate revisionPromptTemplate = new PromptTemplate(STATEMENT_USER_MESSAGE);

        // 1. Execute the heavy LLM call (Parallelizable, network-bound)
        var response = chatClient.prompt()
                .messages(List.of(systemMsg, revisionPromptTemplate.createMessage(Map.of("statementText", userMessage))))
                .call();

        // 2. Synchronize the CPU/Reflection bound JSON Schema parsing (Thread-safe) schema generation tool is not thread-safe.
        synchronized (SCHEMA_LOCK) {
            return response.entity(clazz);
        }
    }
```

Maybe not the prettiest solution, but it works.

Spring Ai does cache schemas, so an alternative could be to init the schemas in a @PostConstuct method.

