@goutamadwant for the example above the conversationHistory() is automatically disabled: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-ai/blob/1a0ada6df5715f518fee6adc40d7d2963bdcd677/spring-ai-client-chat/src/main/java/org/springframework/ai/chat/client/DefaultChatClient.java#L1248
So this can not be to root cause for the above issue. 

However having a general de-duplication logic still makes sense for the cases use sets the ToolCallingAdvisor manually  and do not disable the  conversationHistory() in combination with down stream chat memory

We ran into this with bedrock and patched it with the following

```
class ConversationCacheInterceptor : ExecutionInterceptor {

    override fun modifyRequest(context: Context.ModifyRequest, executionAttributes: ExecutionAttributes): SdkRequest =
        when (val request = context.request()) {
            is ConverseStreamRequest -> request.toBuilder().messages(cacheTail(request.messages())).build()
            is ConverseRequest -> request.toBuilder().messages(cacheTail(request.messages())).build()
            else -> request
        }

    private fun cacheTail(messages: List<Message>): List<Message> {
        val last = messages.lastOrNull() ?: return messages
        val cached = last.toBuilder().content(last.content() + ContentBlock.fromCachePoint(CACHE_POINT)).build()
        return messages.dropLast(1) + cached
    }

    private companion object {
        val CACHE_POINT: CachePointBlock = CachePointBlock.builder().type("default").build()
    }
}
```

Note:
- the class will need to be registered in the application's resources to get picked up
- this is fairly large hammer (applies to all conversations), but for our use case is fine, and needed it to stop the bleeding
- there's most likely a much better solution closer integrated to the Spring AI layer

