@codingkiddo , @Grandys, please don't rush with PRs. 

As @codingkiddo suggested the obvious solution is to always register the ToolCallingAdvisor unless disabled. 
We have already a working draft for this approach but will be interesting to explore for side effects or different ideas.

This looks like the right layer to fix it. One thing I’d watch for is making the preservation rule provider-neutral rather than Gemini-specific.

For OpenAI-compatible endpoints, unknown tool-call metadata can be semantically required even if it is not part of the OpenAI core schema. Gemini’s `extra_content.google.thought_signature` is a good example, but other gateways may attach similar per-tool-call fields for routing, cache markers, safety tokens, or provider-native continuation state.

Suggested regression shape:

- fake OpenAI-compatible provider returns an assistant tool call with root-level additional properties;
- Spring AI executes the tool and sends the follow-up request;
- the replayed assistant tool call still contains those additional properties byte-for-byte, or at least semantically unchanged;
- cover streaming and non-streaming paths if both construct `AssistantMessage.ToolCall` differently;
- cover multiple tool calls so metadata does not get attached to the wrong `tool_call_id`;
- ensure observability/logging redacts values but can still show that provider-specific tool-call metadata was preserved.

Disclosure: I’m associated with/evaluating KiroForge (`https://kiroforge.cloud/v1`), an OpenAI-compatible gateway. From gateway compatibility testing, tool-call round-trip metadata is one of the places where “OpenAI-compatible” clients most often break provider-native semantics, so preserving unknown fields at the tool-call boundary is a much safer default than whitelisting only known OpenAI fields.

