I found a workaround using a custom `ToolCallResultConverter` together with a dedicated wrapper type for markdown responses.

Instead of returning `String` directly, I return a custom record:

```java
public record MarkdownResultRecord(@JsonValue String content) {}
```

Then I provide a custom converter that unwraps that record and returns the raw markdown content:

```java
@Component
public class MarkdownResultConverter implements ToolCallResultConverter {

    @Override
    public String convert(Object result, @Nullable Type var2) {
        if (result instanceof MarkdownResultRecord(String content)) {
            return content;
        }
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Only MarkdownResultRecord is supported");
    }
}
```

And I reference that converter explicitly on the tool method:

```java
@Tool(
    description = "Decode a JWT token and return its header, payload, and signature as markdown. "
        + "Does not verify the signature. Takes the JWT token string as input and returns a formatted "
        + "markdown response with JSON-formatted header and payload, plus the signature string.",
    resultConverter = MarkdownResultConverter.class
)
public MarkdownResultRecord jwtDecode(final String token) {
    ...
}
```

With this setup, the markdown is returned as plain text and the extra quoting in MCP `content[].text` is avoided for this tool.

So there is a workable escape hatch, but I still believe the default handling of plain `String` tool results is surprising when the target field is already MCP text content.

@runkelstein After careful look at this issue, it turns out there is an even simpler way to achieve what you want.
Just write a `NoopToolCallResultConverter` that return the (String) input as-is and use it in your tool.

As far as Spring AI is concerned, the default behavior will continue to be to JSON encode all results. We may later support a mode where the ToolCallback has more knowledge of the user's intention with regard to structure.

